A||||8||DXR_DAD||"87" Headliner||||14||
Z||000000||DXR_DAD||12-06-1999||01:27 AM||||It's just about done, and it looks darn good, for a rookie. The job went well until I was removing the board and broke it, so how do I fix this?? I got the fabric $7.00 a yard nice color gray it matches good also got Elmer's Craft Bond spray glue:<P>Multiple surfaces<BR>Dries fast and clear<BR>Resisrs Bleed-Through<P>It works good on the foam back fabric.<BR>well I need to try somthing to fix the board.<BR>I'll let you guys know.||||reg||14
Z||000001||Patrick||12-06-1999||03:38 AM||||<BR>I had the same problem. I broke mine in two places (on each side of the sunroof opening) when I took it out a couple of years ago.<P>I used a couple of small pieces (4" x 6") of sheet metal bent up to resemble the original contours of the liner. I drilled multiple holes through them, roughed them up real good with sandpaper, and then used a glue gun to stick them to the roof side (obviously) of the liner. It's held up fine ever since.<P>By the way, I also put tape on the sheet metal so it wouldn't squeak against the inside of the roof. Being Canadian, I used hockey tape, but I'm sure duct tape would work just as well. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000002||Carrolles||12-06-1999||06:37 AM||||I've broken several through the years.  I use disposable handy wipe type dust rags as a patch (not the ones with soap).  Just cut a patch large enough to wrap around both sides of the headliner and use the same glue that you use to attach the fabric to both sides.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DXR_DAD||12-06-1999||07:09 PM||||Thanks guys, It's done and looks great, if I do say so myself. I did one dang good job.<BR>These repairs get better and beter. I like not having to go to the rip-off shops. Thanks to the FIERO FOURM!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Greg Piet||12-07-1999||07:32 PM||||I used fiberglass resin to do the mending and it worked well too.  It will never break now!||||reg||1
Z||000005||DXR_DAD||12-07-1999||09:37 PM||||Greg did the fiberglass leave any smell, if so for how long. The glue I used has no smell at all. I did let the headliner sit out over night before I installed it.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Greg Piet||12-09-1999||03:55 PM||||The fiberglass resin was done on a sat night and installed on tuesday.  For a day or two I could smell it lightly in the car, but not after that.   I did however use A LOT of resin!   One of those big cans was used (but under the gallon).  After that I ended up doing a little sanding to smooth out where the fiber broke in 1/2 before putting on the cloth.  So the resin was expensive, and the 3M Super 77 spray adhesive worked great, and 1 yard of JC Whitney nylon tricot in the Med Gray.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DXR_DAD||12-09-1999||06:40 PM||||now my headliner looks so good I need to do something about my sun visors??  any good tips? what about having them recovered, whats the price?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Carrolles||12-10-1999||08:02 AM||||I recovered my sunvisors with the same headliner fabric when I redid the headliner.  It's not hard to do at all.  <BR>The sunvisor is basically a piece of cardboard folded over the support shaft flange with fabric glued on and sewn around the edges.  The thick headliner fabric made it impossible for me to reproduce the pockets my originals had.<BR>I simply layed open the cardboard and glued the fabric on with about 1/2" of overlap to wrap around the edges.  We then simply folded it back over the support frame and my wife sewed the edges shut on a standard Sears sewing machine (very slowly due to the thickness of the seam).<P>They look great snd match the headliner.<P>I'm thinking of opening them up again and installing lighted mirrors from a wreck.||||reg||1
